    text: 'the following notes: Modularity of mind is the notion that the mind is at least partly composed of innate neural structures (modules) that perform fast, necessary tasks. 1983: cognitive scientist Jerry A. Fodor hypothesized that low-level cognitive systems (e.g., perception, language) are modular. In Fodorian modularity, high-level systems (e.g., reasoning) are not modular. 2003: cognitive scientist Peter Carruthers proposed the massive modularity hypothesis (MMH). MMH expands modularity to include all cognitive systems. ’ ’ The student wants to compare Fodor s hypothesis with Carruthers s. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?',
